  :prose
"A number by itself lies. '5' could be 5 meters, 5 seconds, 5 kilograms,
5 dollars, or 5 anythings. In physics simulation this ambiguity is
where most bugs live. You multiply a mass by a velocity thinking
you're getting momentum, but you actually multiplied by a distance
and got momentum-times-time — off by a factor. The sim behaves
weirdly and you don't know why.

Dimensional analysis is the technique of tagging numbers with their
units and letting the computer check that operations are consistent.
Motoi Scheme's `phys/quantity` and family do this. This appendix
walks through ten worked bugs — real errors dimensional analysis
would have caught before you ran the sim.

---

## B.1 phys/quantity and friends

A quantity is a value plus a dimension alist — e.g. `((length 1))` is
metres, `((time 1))` is seconds. `phys/q*` and `phys/q/` combine the
dimensions for you.

```scheme
(define distance (phys/quantity 5 '((length 1))))   ;; 5 metres
(define time     (phys/quantity 2 '((time 1))))     ;; 2 seconds
(define velocity (phys/q/ distance time))
(phys/dimension velocity)
;; => ((length 1) (time -1))   ;; metres per second, correct

(phys/same-dimension? velocity (phys/quantity 3 '((length 1) (time -1))))
;; => #t
```

`phys/q+`, `phys/q*`, `phys/q/` all propagate dimensions and check
compatibility. Adding metres to seconds is an error caught before the
sim ever runs — `phys/same-dimension?` returns `#f` so you can guard:

```scheme
;; Guard instead of blindly adding: are these even the same dimension?
(if (phys/same-dimension? distance time)
    (phys/q+ distance time)
    (list 'refused 'incompatible (phys/dimension distance) (phys/dimension time)))
;; => (refused incompatible ((length 1)) ((time 1)))
```

## B.2 Ten worked bugs

### Bug 1: The 3600 factor.

```scheme
;; WRONG: a bare number hides the units.
(define speed-raw 60)                       ;; kilometres per hour? per second?
(define distance-raw (* speed-raw 30))      ;; 30 later
;; => 1800 ??? "the car went 1800 km in 30 seconds"

;; RIGHT: tag speed as length/time so the dimension of the product is checked.
;; (Convert 60 km/hr to km/s first: 60/3600 = 0.01667 km/s.)
(define speed-q (phys/quantity (/ 60 3600) '((length 1) (time -1))))
(define time-q  (phys/quantity 30 '((time 1))))
(define distance-q (phys/q* speed-q time-q))
(list (phys/dimension distance-q))          ;; => (((length 1)))  -> a length!
```

The dimensions force you to be honest: km/hr is `length * time^-1`,
and multiplying by a time leaves `length`. Do the numeric conversion
(60 km/hr = 0.01667 km/s) at the boundary, then let the dimension
alist confirm the RESULT is a distance, not a distance-times-time.

### Bug 2: Radians vs degrees.

```scheme
;; WRONG:
(sin 30)   ;; the sine of 30 RADIANS = -0.988
;; when you meant the sine of 30 DEGREES = 0.5

;; RIGHT:
(sin (geom/->radians 30))
;; => 0.5
```

The `sin` function takes radians. Passing degrees gives absurd
answers.

### Bug 3: Mass vs weight.

```scheme
;; WRONG: use a weight (a force) where a mass belongs.
;;   (entity/mass! b 150)   ;; treats 150 lb-force as if it were 150 kg
;; mass = weight / g. First convert 150 lbf to newtons: 150 * 4.448 = 667 N.

;; RIGHT: divide force by acceleration -> a mass, and check the dimension.
(define weight (phys/quantity (* 150 4.448) '((mass 1) (length 1) (time -2))))  ;; newtons
(define g      (phys/quantity 9.81 '((length 1) (time -2))))                    ;; m/s^2
(define mass   (phys/q/ weight g))
(list (phys/dimension mass))     ;; => (((mass 1)))  -> a mass, ~68 kg
```

Weight is a force; mass is a mass. Conflating them is a factor-of-g
error, which for Earth is about 10.

### Bug 4: Energy vs power.

```scheme
;; WRONG:
(define bulb-consumption 60)                ;; watts
(define hours-on         8)
(define bill-raw         (* bulb-consumption hours-on))
;; => 480 ... watts? watt-hours? kilowatt-hours?

;; RIGHT: power is energy/time (mass*length^2*time^-3); times a time -> energy.
(define bulb (phys/quantity 60 '((mass 1) (length 2) (time -3))))  ;; watts
(define on   (phys/quantity (* 8 3600) '((time 1))))               ;; 8 hr in seconds
(define usage (phys/q* bulb on))
(list (phys/dimension usage))    ;; => (((mass 1) (length 2) (time -2)))  -> energy (joules)
```

Watts are joules per second. Watt-hours are joules. Kilowatt-hours
are 3.6 million joules. Bills are in kilowatt-hours. Confusing them
costs money.

### Bug 5: Spring constant units.

```scheme
;; k = 100 N/m in physics land. If your sim uses pixels for length
;; and 1/60-second for time, 1 sim-second = 60 sim-frames, and
;; k needs to be rescaled.

;; WRONG:
;;   (world/link! anchor bob 100 100)   ;; k=100 in sim-units, absurdly stiff

;; RIGHT: pre-convert k from N/m to sim-units at the boundary, once.
(define k-physical 100)                 ;; N/m
(define px-per-m   100)                 ;; 100 pixels per metre
(define frames-per-s 60)
;; k has units force/length = mass/time^2; rescale time (frames) and length (px):
(define k-sim (/ k-physical (* frames-per-s frames-per-s)))
;; then use k-sim as the stiffness argument to world/link!
(list 'k-sim k-sim)
```

Whenever your sim uses non-SI units (pixels, frames), you need a
consistent conversion. Do it once at the boundary, not scattered
through your code.

### Bug 6: Angular velocity.

```scheme
;; WRONG: is 360 in degrees/second, radians/second, or rotations/second?
;;   360 rad/s is very fast; 360 deg/s is one revolution per second.

;; RIGHT: decide the unit, then convert to the radians the engine expects.
(define spin-deg-per-s 360)
(define spin-rad-per-s (geom/->radians spin-deg-per-s))
;; => ~6.283 rad/s  (one revolution per second)
(list 'spin spin-rad-per-s)
```

### Bug 7: Gravitational constant.

```scheme
;; The gravitational constant G = 6.674e-11 N*m^2/kg^2 (SI).
;; But your sim uses pixels for length, sim-frames for time...
;; The 'G' you pass to world/attract! is EFFECTIVE G in sim units.

;; If you want realistic orbits at solar-system scale, you need to
;; work out the conversion:
;;    G_sim = G * (pixels/meter)^-3 * (frames/second)^2 * kg-scale
;; A mess. Better: use realistic physical units end-to-end (Appendix I).
```

### Bug 8: Torque vs energy.

```scheme
;; Both have units of N*m. But they are NOT the same physical quantity.
;; Torque is a rotational force. Energy is joules.
;; Motoi's phys/dimension will report the same for both, so you must
;; label at the semantic level:
(define torque (phys/quantity 5 '((mass 1) (length 2) (time -2))))
(define energy (phys/quantity 5 '((mass 1) (length 2) (time -2))))
(phys/same-dimension? torque energy)   ;; => #t  (dimensionally identical!)
;; They share a dimension, so the alist can't tell them apart. Keep the
;; semantic distinction in your own naming; the dimension check won't.
```

### Bug 9: Off by 2*pi in Fourier.

```scheme
;; When you compute a frequency f = omega / (2 pi), and you drop
;; the 2 pi, your natural frequency is off by 6.28. If you tune
;; a resonance chart, you're at the wrong pole.

;; RIGHT: keep the 2*pi explicit. f = omega / (2*pi).
(define omega 6.28)                       ;; rad/s
(define f (/ omega (* 2 3.14159)))        ;; cycles/s = Hz
;; => ~1 Hz
(list 'frequency-hz f)
```

### Bug 10: Newtons in a screen-pixel-per-frame world.

```scheme
;; You want a force of 10 N applied to a 1 kg mass at 60 fps.
;; a = F/m = 10 m/s^2.
;; In sim frames: a = 10 (m/s^2) / (60 frames/s)^2 * (100 px/m)
;;              = 10 / 3600 * 100 = 0.278 px/frame^2

;; WRONG:
;;   (world/impulse! b 10 0)   ;; 10 taken as px/frame impulse. WAY too much.

;; RIGHT: convert 10 N acting for one frame into a sim-unit acceleration.
;;   a = F/m = 10 m/s^2 ; in sim: /(60 frames/s)^2 * (100 px/m)
(define F 10)   (define m 1)
(define a-si  (/ F m))                             ;; 10 m/s^2
(define a-sim (* (/ a-si (* 60 60)) 100))          ;; ~0.278 px/frame^2
;; then apply a-sim as the per-frame acceleration in sim units
(list 'a-sim a-sim)
```

---

## B.3 A checked pipeline

Set units at the boundaries; let the middle be dimensionless. Or
carry units through everywhere. Both are fine; MIXING them is
dangerous.

Alfred's discipline: at the top of every simulation file, declare
your unit system.

```scheme
;;; Units: length = pixel; time = frame (60 fps); mass = kg;
;;;        force = kg*px/frame^2; energy = kg*px^2/frame^2
;;; Conversions: 100 px = 1 m; 60 frames = 1 s.
```

Every entering quantity gets converted at the entry point:

```scheme
(define (m->px m) (* m 100))
(define (s->frame s) (* s 60))
```

Every exiting quantity gets converted at the exit point:

```scheme
(define (px->m p) (/ p 100))
(define (frame->s f) (/ f 60))
```

Inside the sim, everything is in the sim's native units. This is
what Motoi Scheme uses internally, and it makes the sim code shorter
and faster while keeping the boundary conversions honest.

---

## B.4 The takeaway

Half the physics bugs in games are unit-mismatch bugs, and the
programmer never sees the mismatch — the sim just behaves oddly.
`phys/dimension` and `phys/same-dimension?` catch these BEFORE you
run. Ten seconds of dimensional check saves an hour of 'why does my
pendulum swing so fast.'

Related:
- Chapter 1: raw sim units, no dimensional checking
- Chapter 11: uncertainty propagation through RLC formulas
- Appendix I: SI units for solar-system-scale physics")
